-## Subtopics
-
-### Early Ray Termination
-A technique to stop ray marching when accumulated opacity exceeds a threshold, reducing computation. This method implements it via the termination check pass and early z-test [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Empty-Space Skipping
-A technique to skip regions of the volume with no relevant data. This method integrates it by discarding fragments that have already terminated or passed through empty space, using the z-test to skip shader execution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Dependent Texture Fetches:** Hardware limitations (e.g., on ATI 9700) restrict the number of dependent texture fetches. The method must minimize dynamic texture lookups within the shader [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Floating Point Precision:** Use of floating-point textures is essential for accurate accumulation of color and opacity, especially for negative values or high dynamic range [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Depth Buffer Clearing:** The depth buffer must be cleared appropriately before the termination check pass to ensure the `GREATER` test functions as intended. Not given in the sources.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of passes and the number of fragments that survive the early z-test. The multi-pass approach adds overhead but reduces per-fragment work for terminated rays.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires additional 2D textures for accumulation and direction storage. The size is proportional to the screen resolution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Performance gains up to a factor of 3 for typical renditions of volumetric data sets on the ATI 9700 graphics card [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective integration of acceleration techniques; exploits hardware parallelism; efficient communication between passes via texture render targets [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires hardware support for texture render targets, dependent texture fetches, and early z-test; limited to DirectX 9-level hardware features [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When targeting older programmable graphics hardware (e.g., ATI 9700) and needing to integrate early ray termination and empty-space skipping efficiently [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** On modern hardware with more advanced shading capabilities and memory management where single-pass or other acceleration techniques might be more efficient [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and the fixed number of passes. Early termination reduces the effective number of passes for opaque regions.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for ray direction textures, accumulation textures, and volume data. The multi-pass nature requires additional memory for intermediate render targets.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Demonstrated performance gains up to a factor of 3 for typical renditions of volumetric data sets on the ATI 9700 graphics card [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Effective integration of early ray termination and empty-space skipping on hardware that supports early z-tests and depth replace. Exploits parallelism of fragment processors.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires specific hardware features (Pixel Shader 2.0, depth replace, texture render targets). Limited by the number of dependent texture fetches on older hardware. Fixed number of passes may lead to unnecessary computation for rays that terminate early if not managed well.
+*   **When to Use:** When targeting older programmable graphics hardware (DirectX 9 era) and needing real-time or interactive volume rendering with acceleration techniques.
+*   **When Not to Use:** On modern hardware with more advanced ray tracing capabilities or single-pass optimized ray marching techniques that do not require multi-pass accumulation.
 
